# Guide placement from mesh topology

- Preserve an unsaved artist scene in a versioned native snapshot before fitting.
- Inspect the complete approved reference: body, limb, hand, neck and facial
  joint positions, hierarchy and local frames, as well as its guide curves.
  Compare anatomical loop roles; do not copy another character's dimensions.
- Joint centers come from the intended edge loop or explicit component patch.
  Keep the distinction between a bounding-box center and an arithmetic centroid.
  A selected face band can intentionally place a center between two edge rows.
  Do not move such a joint to one band boundary merely because it is closer.
- A cluster handle's world rotate pivot is the useful position, not its transform
  translation, which can be zero. In Maya 2026 inspect component tags on the
  original geometry and the deformer's componentTagExpression to recover exact
  selected edges/faces. An absent deformerSet is valid. Default nonzero weights
  across the whole mesh do not identify the component-tag membership.
- Fit on the requested resolution. A low-resolution artist selection can define
  the intended anatomy, but record the corresponding actual High Res edges.
- Lip, eyelid and socket linear guides use one CV per ordered mesh vertex, with every consecutive
  pair connected by an actual selected mesh edge. Do not project donor CVs to the
  surface, resample, smooth or rebuild them to invent the source contour.
- Upper/lower lips share both endpoints and the same CV count. Each upper/lower
  eyelid pair does the same. For this user's convention CV[0] is their identical
  corner with the largest WORLD X coordinate. This means global +X, not absolute
  X: the right eye starts at its inner corner and the left at its outer corner.
  An older reference export with another order does not override that request.
- Sockets use the intended outer orbital edge loop, closed by repeating the
  first vertex as the last CV when following Edward's linear-polyline format.
  Eyebrows follow the longitudinal edge chain of the actual eyebrow mesh when
  present. Attach that route to the BODY surface, not to the separate eyebrow
  mesh. Keep CV[0] nearest the world centerline (minimum absolute world X).
  Project the source eyebrow chain to body UVs rather than forcing it to jump
  between body edge rows and introducing a stair-step in the brow arch. Preserve
  the actual eyebrow source edges and the body attachment UVs separately.
- Three-joint arms: root has free T/R, middle has only local TX and RY, terminal
  has only local TX with all rotations zero. Legs follow TX/RZ at the middle
  and TX only at the terminal. Keep unused translations, rotateAxis and
  jointOrient zero; do not hide extra bend axes in jointOrient. Preserve the
  intended world anatomical anchors by orienting the root frame and expressing
  the bend on the single allowed axis. Preserve unaffected descendant frames.
- Preserve live artist corrections as the authoritative source for a requested
  mirror. Girl's left hip uses RX=90/RZ=-90, retaining its authored RY; its native
  behavior mirror uses RX=-90/RZ=90 on R, negative child TX and the same knee RZ.
  Mirror the full leg branch, including ball/tip, and preserve the left matrices
  and channels exactly. Do not refit the corrected source from old loop data.
- Shoulder X follows the upper arm. For Girl's side-view up convention, L has
  RX=0 and the behavior mirror on R has RX=180; signed Y has no world-Z lean in
  the side projection. Retain elbow bend channels and independent finger frames.
- A subsequent artist-edited shoulder frame is authoritative. When the artist
  explicitly keeps BOTH its position and orientation and retains elbow TX/RY,
  fit elbow TX to the closest loop-center projection on shoulder X, then fit
  wrist TX and elbow RY in the shoulder XZ plane. Do not change shoulder, add RZ,
  or hide an extra degree of freedom in jointOrient/offsetParentMatrix. Record
  the unavoidable residual and do not claim exact loop centering. Girl v005's
  accepted wrist residual is 1.87035069 cm; both shoulders are preserved.
- A marked front edge can specify the spine endpoint's HEIGHT rather than a
  front-surface pivot. Record the exact source edge chain and centerline vertex;
  retain the artist's spine root frame and redistribute uniform child TY to the
  marked height. Center chest at the world midpoint between the spine endpoint
  and the bilateral clavicle center, with X=0. Girl v004's marked edge is the
  Top_high front chain through vertex 15 (Y=122.315338 scene units).
- Spine and neck use only local TY between joints, with uniform spacing and
  zero child rotations. The root places and orients each straight chain while
  retaining the intended endpoints. Ear guides run from the lobe to the helix
  tip with the third guide exactly halfway between them on one local axis
  (Y in the existing ear module).
- Foot pivot locators follow the actual foot/shoe mesh perimeter in X/Z and
  stay at local and world Y=0. Native surface attachments can drive X/Z while
  TY remains zero and locked. Verify an actual mesh move and restore it.
  Native scene attachments are distinct from the evaluated static matrices/CVs
  saved by the existing .guides export format.
- Foot joint orientation is a later explicit exception to the earlier terminal
  ankle zero-rotation convention: preserve ankle/ball centers, aim positive X
  from ankle to ball and from ball to tip, and use positive world Y as the up
  reference, projected perpendicular to the aim. Apply positive X/Y on BOTH
  sides for this foot request. Place each tip exactly at its tip locator, with
  local TX only and zero rotation. Keep jointOrient/rotateAxis zero and preserve
  hip/knee frames. Ankle rotations are editable; ball/tip translations use TX.
- Preserve the chosen vertex IDs, edge IDs and component sets so an artist can
  select and inspect the actual source topology afterward. Record any ambiguous
  anatomical patch without silently assigning it to an unrelated guide.
- Preserve the artist's clusters, model points and unaffected guide positions.
  Save a new native scene and .guides version. Verify connectivity, exact vertex
  positions, paired endpoint order and a native reopen before presenting it.

Measured reference: Edward_v013.guides on Edward_v003.ma. Its left facial curves
and lips coincide with consecutive cabeza_high vertices to about 1.53e-5 scene
units. Girl v002 records its exact source edges and the artist's nine clusters
under assets/Girl/validation/. This is placement evidence, not rig validation.
